      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "How to memoize any recursion"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Find the state**: the arguments that change between calls. For fib it's just `n`; for a grid it might be `(row, col)`; for a knapsack `(item, capacity)`.",
        "**Make a cache keyed by the state**: an array if the state is a small integer or two, otherwise a hash map.",
        "**Check first**: at the top of the function (after the base cases), return the cached value if there is one.",
        "**Store before returning**: compute the answer exactly as before, save it in the cache, then return it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "fib-memo",
      "caption": "The first call to each fib(k) recurses and fills the memo; every later call is a lookup."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function fib(n, memo = new Map()) {\n  if (n <= 1) return n;\n  if (memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n);   // computed before: reuse it\n  const value = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);\n  memo.set(n, value);\n  return value;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "from functools import cache\n\n@cache                                    # remembers every fib(k) it has returned\ndef fib(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "Map<Integer, Integer> memo = new HashMap<>();\n\nint fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    if (memo.containsKey(n)) return memo.get(n);   // computed before: reuse it\n    int value = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n    memo.put(n, value);\n    return value;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "unordered_map<int, int> memo;\n\nint fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    if (memo.count(n)) return memo[n];    // computed before: reuse it\n    int value = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n    memo[n] = value;\n    return value;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In Python, `@functools.cache` (or `@lru_cache(None)`) memoizes any function whose arguments are hashable — one line. In other languages, write the check and the store yourself."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The order it visits the subproblems"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Memoization still walks the call tree **depth-first**, but each state's subtree is explored only the first time; afterwards the state is a single lookup. So the walk really visits a **DAG of states**: fib(5) → fib(4) → fib(3) → fib(2) → fib(1), then returns upward, filling the memo from the **smallest** states first, exactly in the order their answers become available. You never decide the order yourself — the recursion finds it."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "How much memory"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**The memo**: one entry per distinct state — O(n) for fib, O(m · n) for a grid state.",
        "**The call stack**: as deep as the longest chain of calls — O(n) for fib. This is memoization's weak spot: Python's default limit is 1,000 frames, so `fib(5000)` with `@cache` crashes with a RecursionError even though the work is tiny. Bottom-up tabulation (next lesson) has no stack at all."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Common mistakes"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**A key that misses part of the state** — if the answer depends on `(i, remaining)`, caching on `i` alone returns wrong answers.",
        "**Sharing one memo between different inputs** — a memo built for one array is wrong for another; create it per call of the main function.",
        "**Python mutable defaults** — `def f(n, memo={})` keeps the same dict across calls; fine for fib, a bug when the inputs change.",
        "**Caching before the base cases** — base cases can stay plain returns; only store computed answers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Key takeaways"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Memoization = the plain recursion + a cache keyed by the **state**.",
        "Each distinct state is solved once: time = states × work per state.",
        "It explores only the states actually needed, in the order the recursion reaches them.",
        "Memory = the cache + the call stack; deep recursion can overflow."
      ]
    }
  ],
